<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # This Is My Favorite. – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.2em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> A Walk in the Neighborhood</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> This Is My Favorite.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading1: Outline key information in simple factual texts on familiar subjects. reading2: Comprehend instructions for classroom projects and crafts. phonemicAwareness: Decode English graphemes and phonemes using knowledge of word parts, syllabification, and phonemic awareness.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can read and outline key information about places in the neighborhood in short texts using superlatives (e.g., 'The library is the best place to read.'). Can comprehend instructions and highlight phrases describing familiar places for a classroom project (e.g., 'The park has swings.'). Can blend spoken onsets and rhymes to form one-syllable and two-syllable words (e.g., /b/ and /ɪg/ in 'big' and /b/ /ɪg/ /g/ /əv/) in bigger.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and initiates a "Neighborhood Favorites Review." The teacher shows large flashcards or pictures of different neighborhood places (e.g., park, library, school, store, playground, building, street, tree, flower, path) previously introduced. Students point to a picture and say, "This is the [park]. It is [fun/beautiful]." The teacher guides them to introduce "favorite" by asking, "What is your favorite place?" and modeling the answer, "My favorite place is the [park] because it is [fun]."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a simple sentence strip or reads from the board: "The park is the best place to play because it has a big playground." The teacher slowly reads the sentence, pointing to each word. The teacher models the pronunciation of /b/ in 'best' and 'big' and /f/ in 'favorite,' emphasizing clear articulation. The teacher also demonstrates how to blend the onset /b/ and rhyme /ɪg/ to form 'big'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'the best' good or bad?" (Expected answer: "Good!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why do we say 'because'?" (Expected answer: "To tell why" or "To give a reason.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "A Walk in the Neighborhood."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short factual text titled "Our Neighborhood: Favorite Places." The text consists of 3-4 short paragraphs, each describing a different neighborhood place (e.g., 'The Community Park', 'The Quiet Library', 'The Busy Supermarket'), using the target vocabulary (park, library, building, street, fun, beautiful, quiet, busy, best) and grammar (superlatives, simple present + because). Each paragraph includes a picture of the place. Example sentence: "The Community Park is the best place to play because it is big and has many trees." or "The Quiet Library is the best place to read because it is calm and has many exciting books."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Outline key information in simple factual texts on familiar subjects (neighborhood places) and identify reasons for preferences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> The teacher asks students to read the title and look at the pictures. "What is this text about?" (Expected: "Our neighborhood places.") "What kind of places do you see?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Read-Through:</b> The teacher reads the text aloud, pointing to the words. Students listen and follow along.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second Read-Through (Guided):</b> Students read silently or in pairs. The teacher gives them a simple matching task: "Match the place name (Park, Library, Supermarket) with its description." (e.g., "This place is quiet." -> Library)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Finding Key Information:</b> The teacher asks, "Which place is the *best* for playing?" or "Which place is the *best* for reading?" Students point to or name the place from the text.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice reading and identifying superlatives ('the best', 'the worst') and understanding 'because' to explain opinions about neighborhood places. They will also practice decoding and blending words like 'big', 'bigger', 'biggest'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – "Superlative & Reason Hunt":</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with the "Our Neighborhood: Favorite Places" text. Students individually read the text again.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher instructs students to underline all words with the /b/ sound (e.g., 'best', 'big', 'building') and the /f/ sound (e.g., 'favorite', 'fun', 'flower').</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students circle all superlative adjectives (e.g., "the best") they find in the text.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students highlight all sentences that use the word "because" to explain why a place is preferred.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher checks answers as a class, guiding students to read the sentences they highlighted.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice – "Missing Words in My Neighborhood":</b> The teacher provides a cloze activity based on sentences from the text, but with key vocabulary or superlatives missing.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example sentences: "The ______ is the best place to play because it is ______." (Options: park / fun, quiet, small) "I like this ______ because it has beautiful ______." (Options: street / trees, buildings, paths)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the sentences and choose the correct word from a word bank to complete them, focusing on comprehension and appropriate vocabulary.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher guides students to read the completed sentences aloud, paying attention to pronunciation of words like 'big' and 'bigger', connecting to phonemic awareness practice.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Check – "Reading Preferences":</b> In pairs, students receive two new, very short descriptions of a neighborhood place (e.g., "This place has many swings and slides. It is the best place to run and play because it is big and fun." / "This place has many books. It is the best place to read because it is quiet and calm.").
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A reads the first description aloud to Student B. Student B listens and points to the corresponding picture (e.g., park, library) from a set of flashcards.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B then reads the second description aloud to Student A, who identifies the picture.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They quickly discuss: "Why is it the best place?" based on the 'because' clause in the text.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "A Walk in the Neighborhood."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "Reading Project Instructions for My Favorite Place"</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher explains that students will start a small project about their favorite neighborhood place. The task for today is to *read and understand the instructions*.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students receive a worksheet with clear, numbered instructions for a simple craft or drawing project (e.g., creating a "My Favorite Place" mini-poster or drawing). The instructions are written at A1.2 level, using familiar vocabulary and simple present tense.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example instructions:
                        <ol style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Read the instructions carefully.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Think about your favorite place in the neighborhood. (e.g., park, playground, street, building)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Draw a picture of your favorite place on your paper.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Write two sentences about your place. Use "the best" and "because." (e.g., "The park is the best place to play because it is fun.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Share your drawing and sentences with a friend.</li>
                        </ol>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student Action:</b> Students read the instructions independently. Then, they underline or highlight all action verbs (e.g., 'read', 'think', 'draw', 'write', 'share') and circle the key phrases "the best" and "because." This demonstrates their comprehension of instructions for a craft.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher walks around, observing students' reading and highlighting, offering support for decoding unfamiliar words if needed.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist of student's ability to read and outline key information and comprehend instructions. A quick written quiz for comprehension.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students receive a new, short, simple paragraph describing one neighborhood place (e.g., "My school is a big building. It is the best place to learn because my friends are here and the teachers are fun. We play in the playground every day.")</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the paragraph.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They answer 2-3 multiple-choice comprehension questions about the text (e.g., "What is the best place in the text? a) park b) school c) library", "Why is it the best place? a) It is big. b) Friends are there. c) It has flowers.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They also underline the superlative (e.g., "the best") and circle the word "because" in the text.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in answering reading comprehension questions (outlining key information).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct identification and highlighting of superlatives.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct identification and circling of 'because' as a discourse marker.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effort in decoding familiar words and word parts.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket with two sentences:</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I read about ____________________."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It was (easy/a little difficult/difficult) to read about 'the best' places."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7-8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading today helped me understand the most?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did I read today? Which was my favorite word?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this reading lesson? Students successfully identified key information and 'because' statements. The phonemic awareness practice for /b/ and /f/ was well-received during reading aloud.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students still struggled with decoding longer words or unfamiliar adjectives. More practice with chunking words into syllables during reading might be beneficial. Ensure visual aids are consistently available.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will use the project instructions they read today to *talk* about their favorite neighborhood places and the reasons why, collaborating with peers and using the target vocabulary and grammatical structures.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>